            ("p", "Short version: <strong>promoting from within is not the problem. A pipeline that closes almost entirely is.</strong> The practical line sits somewhere around one in four to one in three senior roles being filled from outside. Below that, an organization gradually stops importing new reference points, and calm becomes the main symptom."),

            ("h2", "What infrastructure inbreeding actually means"),
            ("p", "Infrastructure inbreeding is the state where most of the people who decide how a company works learned how a company works <em>at that company</em>. Not a hiring policy — an accumulated outcome. Nobody signs off on it. It happens because internal promotion is cheaper, faster, less risky per decision, and easier to defend in a room."),
            ("p", "Each individual promotion is defensible. The aggregate is what does the damage: after enough of them, the entire senior layer shares one reference set. The same escalation habits. The same idea of what \"normal\" looks like. The same blind spots — held with the confidence of consensus, because everyone in the room genuinely agrees."),
            ("p", "That last part is what makes it hard to see from the inside. Inbreeding doesn't feel like a lack of diversity. It feels like alignment."),

            ("h2", "Why internal promotion is still the right default"),
            ("p", "It would be easy — and wrong — to read this as an argument for hiring outsiders into every senior seat. Internal promotion earns its place:"),
